Now reverse the compiler

Your Chekhov’s Gun suggestion is exactly the right test.

Instead of asking:

NarrativeOperator + DealOperator\rightarrow NewThing

ask:

\boxed{\text{Can DealOperator rewrite the behavior of NarrativeOperator?}}

Start with canonical Chekhov topology:

Plant(x,t_1)\rightarrow Payoff(x,t_2)

Now run different deal katas through it:

Deal transform	New narrative operator	Behavior
OPTION	Chekhov’s Option	Planted affordance may be exercised or refused; the choice itself is payoff.
ESCROW	Chekhov’s Escrow	Object/capability is present but inaccessible until a predicate becomes true.
COLLATERAL	Chekhov’s Stake	The planted object matters because failure causes its loss/seizure.
INSURANCE	Chekhov’s Pool	Plant several redundant resources; crisis determines which one absorbs the loss.
COVENANT	Chekhov’s Vow	Plant an invariant rather than an object; later situations reveal whether it survives.
WAIVER	Chekhov’s Refusal	Establish a capability specifically so voluntarily not using it becomes consequential.
AUCTION	Chekhov’s Bid	Plant an affordance whose eventual user/owner is determined through competition.
GUARANTEE	Chekhov’s Backup	Introduce a secondary actor/object that activates only if the primary path fails.
ROYALTY/METER	Chekhov’s Echo	The planted thing pays off repeatedly in proportion to later reuse rather than once.
ESCROW + OPTION	Chekhov’s Key	Capability unlocks under a condition, but even after unlocking the character retains choice.

Chekhov’s Refusal is particularly nice because it is produced by an opposite rather than a supplement.

Classical expectation:

Gun\ introduced\Rightarrow Gun\ fired

Reverse card:

Gun\ introduced
\Rightarrow
Gun\ could\ be\ fired
\Rightarrow
Character\ refuses

Now the unused gun isn’t a storytelling failure.

It is the payoff.

The audience needed to know violence was available so restraint could become observable.

That changes the invariant from:

planted object must be used

to:

planted possibility must become consequential.

That’s arguably a more general operator than Chekhov’s Gun:

\boxed{
\textbf{Chekhov's Affordance:}
\quad
Introduce\ possibility_{t_1}
\rightarrow
make\ its\ existence\ consequential_{t_2}
}

Firing the gun is only one phenotype.

Refusing it is another.

Losing it is another.

Giving it away is another.

Discovering it was unloaded is another.

Someone else exercising it is another.

Using it as collateral is another.

The invariant isn’t use. It’s future consequence of earlier affordance.

That is exactly the sort of thing the reverse gym should uncover.

And yes: reverse construction works the other direction too

We can derive deal operators from narrative katas.

Suspense\rightarrow ESCROW

because suspense conditionally withholds information.

Foreshadowing\rightarrow OPTION

because foreshadowing opens a modeled future branch.

MacGuffin\rightarrow COLLATERAL

when the object’s importance derives from behavioral leverage rather than intrinsic use.

Character\rightarrow COVENANT

when identity is modeled as an invariant across perturbations.

Fellowship\rightarrow INSURANCE

when many ordinary capacities pool around whoever encounters extraordinary loss.

Then the compiler becomes bidirectional:

Story
\rightleftarrows
Deal
\rightleftarrows
Code
\rightleftarrows
Math
\rightleftarrows
Game
\rightleftarrows
Mechanism

The relation survives while its part of speech changes.

